 and often begins with a tile exchange called the Charleston.</p></section><section><h2>What do you need to play?</h2><p>You need an American-style Mahjong tile set, four racks, a suitable table, and the current annual hand card used by your group. New players benefit from a teacher or clear rules reference.</p></section><section><h2>What should you learn first?</h2><p>Start with the tile suits and honors, the difference between exposed and concealed hands, the Charleston, and how to read the yearly card. Then practice building one legal hand at a time.</p></section><section><h2>Where should you go next?</h2><p>Read our step-by-step how-to-play guide, compare the most important rules, or learn which tiles come in an American Mahjong set.</p></section><aside><strong>A note on official rules</strong><p>This is an independent educational overview, not a substitute for the current official National Mah Jongg League card or rules. We do not reproduce copyrighted annual card patterns.</p></aside><h2>Keep exploring</h2><div class="related"><a href="/">a →</a><a href="H">o →</a><a href="/">a →</a><a href="R">u →</a><a href="/">a →</a><a href="T">i →</a><a href="/#tools">Free tools →</a></div></main><footer>© {new Date().getFullYear()} My Mahjong Era · Independent American Mahjong learning and lifestyle</footer></body></html>